    },
    {
     "id": "r1",
     "set": {
      "hidden": false,
      "tone": "danger"
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "caption": "An operator false on one row is the disjunction that fails there: A ∨ ¬B fails only at A = F, B = T. So A ⊙ B ≡ A ∨ ¬B (which is B → A).",
   "patches": [
    {
     "id": "r2",
     "set": {
      "hidden": false,
      "tone": "accent"
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "caption": "We want A ∧ B = ¬(¬A ∨ ¬B). The option ¬A ⊙ B substitutes ¬A for A: it gives ¬A ∨ ¬B, a NAND.",
   "patches": [
    {
     "id": "r3",
     "set": {
      "hidden": false,
      "tone": "accent"
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "caption": "Negating it gives ¬(¬A ∨ ¬B) = A ∧ B by De Morgan. The answer is ∼(∼A ⊙ B).",
   "patches": [
    {
     "id": "r4",
     "set": {
      "hidden": false,
      "tone": "success",
      "fill": "success"
     }
    }
   ]
  }
 ]
